            <ArticleTitle>Mobious syndrome: MR findings.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>502-505</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.4103/0971-3026.195790</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Möbius syndrome is an extremely rare congenital disorder. We report a case of Möbius syndrome in a 2-year-old girl with bilateral convergent squint and left-sided facial weakness. The characteristic magnetic resonance imaging (MRI) findings of Möbius syndrome, which include absent bilateral abducens nerves and absent left facial nerve, were noted. In addition, there was absence of left anterior inferior cerebellar artery (AICA) and absence of bilateral facial colliculi. Clinical features, etiology, and imaging findings are discussed.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
